= 1\. Introduction
== 1\.1. Background

1\. This procedure provides steps to estimate the heat lost from a run of insulated pipework over a reporting period, from the measured surface temperature of the insulation and the ambient temperature of the space the run passes through.


2\. It is intended for operators who report thermal losses of a distribution network against a baseline and who hold measurements at the segment level only.


3\. Earlier versions of this procedure treated the surface coefficient as a single default. Since version 02.0 the radiative part is calculated from the emissivity of the cladding, which matters for bright metal cladding in cold spaces, where the default overstated the loss by up to a fifth.

= 2\. Scope, applicability and entry into force
== 2\.1. Scope and applicability

4\. This procedure applies to runs of pipework carrying a heated fluid where every segment is wrapped in an insulation of known thickness and conductivity, and where the surface temperature of each segment is measured at least monthly.


5\. The procedure does not apply to buried pipework, to segments whose insulation is wet or damaged, or to runs shorter than twenty metres in total.


6\. Where a run passes through more than one space, the operator shall split it at the boundary and treat each part as a run of its own, with the ambient temperature of the space it passes through. A fitting at the boundary belongs to the part upstream of it.

== 2\.2. Entry into force

7\. The date of entry into force is the date of publication of this version by the Office. It applies to reporting periods starting on or after that date.

= 3\. Definitions

8\. The definitions contained in the glossary of the Office apply. A segment is a straight length of pipe between two fittings; a run is an ordered sequence of segments.


9\. The surface temperature of a segment is the temperature of the outer face of its insulation or cladding, measured at the middle of the segment. The ambient temperature is the air temperature of the space at mid-height, away from heat sources.

= 4\. Parameters

10\. This procedure provides steps to determine the following parameters:

