= 5\. Calculation procedure
== 5\.1. Heat loss from a pipe run

11\. The heat lost from pipe run k in year y is the sum over its segments of the product of the outer surface area, the surface heat transfer coefficient and the temperature difference to the ambient, over the hours of operation, as follows:

#grid(columns: (1fr, auto), align: (center + horizon, right + horizon), $ Q_(L,k,y) = sum_(i) A_(i,k,y) times U_(i,y) times Delta T_(i,y) times t_(y) $, [Equation (1)])

Where:

#table(columns: (auto, auto, 1fr), stroke: none, inset: (x: 4pt, y: 3pt),
  [$Q_(L,k,y)$], [=], [Heat lost from pipe run k in year y (GJ/yr)],
  [$A_(i,k,y)$], [=], [Outer surface area of the insulation of segment i of pipe run k in year y (m2)],
  [$U_(i,y)$], [=], [Surface heat transfer coefficient of segment i in year y (W/m2 K)],
  [$Delta T_(i,y)$], [=], [Difference between the surface temperature of segment i and the ambient temperature in year y (K)],
  [$t_(y)$], [=], [Hours of operation of the run in year y (h)],
)
== 5\.2. Surface heat transfer coefficient

12\. The surface heat transfer coefficient combines convection and radiation from the outer face of the insulation. Where no site measurement of the convective part is available, the default convective coefficient of the Office applies and the radiative part is calculated from the emissivity of the cladding, as follows:#footnote[The default convective coefficient is published in the Office's table of defaults and revised annually.]

#grid(columns: (1fr, auto), align: (center + horizon, right + horizon), $ U_(i,y) = h_(i,y) + 4 times epsilon_(i) times sigma times T_(s,i,y)^3 $, [Equation (2)])

Where:

#table(columns: (auto, auto, 1fr), stroke: none, inset: (x: 4pt, y: 3pt),
  [$U_(i,y)$], [=], [Surface heat transfer coefficient of segment i in year y (W/m2 K)],
  [$h_(i,y)$], [=], [Convective heat transfer coefficient at the surface of segment i in year y (W/m2 K)],
  [$epsilon_(i)$], [=], [Emissivity of the cladding of segment i (dimensionless)],
  [$sigma$], [=], [Stefan-Boltzmann constant (W/m2 K4)],
  [$T_(s,i,y)$], [=], [Surface temperature of segment i in year y (K)],
)

13\. The temperature difference of a segment is the difference between its surface temperature and the ambient temperature of the space:

#grid(columns: (1fr, auto), align: (center + horizon, right + horizon), $ Delta T_(i,y) = T_(s,i,y) - T_(a,y) $, [Equation (3)])

Where:

#table(columns: (auto, auto, 1fr), stroke: none, inset: (x: 4pt, y: 3pt),
  [$Delta T_(i,y)$], [=], [Difference between the surface temperature of segment i and the ambient temperature in year y (K)],
  [$T_(s,i,y)$], [=], [Surface temperature of segment i in year y (K)],
  [$T_(a,y)$], [=], [Ambient temperature of the space in year y (K)],
)
== 5\.3. Annual total and uncertainty allowance

14\. The heat lost from all runs is the sum of the losses of the individual runs. The reported figure carries an uncertainty allowance, as follows:

#grid(columns: (1fr, auto), align: (center + horizon, right + horizon), $ Q_(U,y) = (1 + u_(y)) times sum_(k) Q_(L,k,y) $, [Equation (4)])

Where:

#table(columns: (auto, auto, 1fr), stroke: none, inset: (x: 4pt, y: 3pt),
  [$Q_(U,y)$], [=], [Heat loss reported with the uncertainty allowance in year y (GJ/yr)],
  [$u_(y)$], [=], [Uncertainty allowance in year y (fraction)],
  [$Q_(L,k,y)$], [=], [Heat lost from pipe run k in year y (GJ/yr)],
)

15\. The uncertainty allowance is determined by the measurement arrangement of the run, as follows:


#grid(columns: (2em, 1fr), [(a)], [Where every segment carries a calibrated surface sensor read at least weekly, the allowance is 0.05;])
#v(3pt)
#grid(columns: (2em, 1fr), [(b)], [Where the surface temperature is measured monthly with a hand-held instrument, the allowance is 0.10;])
#v(3pt)
#grid(columns: (2em, 1fr), [(c)], [In all other cases the allowance is 0.20, and the operator shall state the arrangement in the report.])
#v(3pt)

